---
import Layout from '@/components/Layout.astro';
import { getGuide, guideJsonLd } from '@/lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('/guides/base64-explained');
---

<Layout
  title={guide.title}
  description={guide.description}
  path={guide.path}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[guideJsonLd(guide)]}
  narrow
>
  <article class="prose-tool max-w-prose">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[rgb(var(--text))] sm:text-3xl">
      {guide.title}
    </h1>

    <p>
      Base64 is not encryption, compression or a secret code — it is a way of writing raw bytes
      using only 64 printable ASCII characters. It exists because many channels can carry text but
      not arbitrary bytes: an email body, a JSON string, a URL, or an attribute in old HTML.
      Whenever you must squeeze binary data into one of those channels, Base64 is the standard way
      to do it.
    </p>

    <h2>What the encoding actually does</h2>
    <p>
      Bytes are eight bits, but the 64 Base64 characters only need six bits each. The encoder reads
      the input three bytes at a time (24 bits) and splits those 24 bits into four groups of six,
      then maps each six-bit group to one of the 64 alphabet characters (<code>A–Z a–z 0–9 + /</code
      >). If fewer than three bytes are left at the end, it pads the result with one or two <code
        >=</code
      > characters so the output stays a multiple of four.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the string <code>Toolbench</code> is not "encrypted" by this — it is re-expressed. Decoding runs
      the same mapping in reverse: skip the padding, turn the letters back into six-bit values and stitch
      them back into bytes.
    </p>

    <h2>The ~33% size overhead</h2>
    <p>
      Because three input bytes become four output characters, Base64 always makes things bigger:
      every three bytes become four characters, so the size grows by 4⁄3, or about 33%. A 3 MB file
      becomes roughly 4 MB of text. Gzip can reclaim much of that for compressible input, but for
      already-compressed data (a JPEG, a PNG, a ZIP) the overhead stays close to the full third.
    </p>
    <p>
      That growth is why you should think before Base64-ing a binary blob "for storage". If the
      destination actually accepts bytes — a file system, an HTTP body — send the bytes. Base64 is
      for text-only channels, not as a general-purpose container.
    </p>

    <h2>When to reach for it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Data URIs</strong> in HTML or CSS (<code>data:image/png;base64,…</code>) so a small
        asset ships inside the document instead of as a separate request.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Email attachments</strong>, where the transport is plain text.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Embedding a binary payload in JSON</strong> — JSON has no byte type, so Base64 is the
        agreed convention.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>JWTs and API tokens</strong>, which are Base64url text on the wire (see below).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The URL-safe variant</h2>
    <p>
      The standard alphabet includes <code>+</code> and <code>/</code>, which mean "space" and path
      separator in URLs and filenames, so the RFC 4648 §5 alphabet swaps them for <code>-</code> and
      <code>_</code> and drops the trailing <code>=</code> padding. That variant is called base64url and
      is what you see inside a JSON Web Token: the three dot-separated segments of a JWT are all unpadded
      base64url. The safe rule is: if the value might appear in a URL, a query string or a filename, use
      the URL-safe alphabet and no padding.
    </p>

    <h2>Unicode caveat</h2>
    <p>
      Base64 operates on bytes, so text must be turned into bytes first — normally UTF-8. The naive
      browser pair <code>btoa</code>/<code>atob</code> throws on anything outside Latin-1; the correct
      path is to UTF-8-encode first. This is exactly why a quick online encoder returns garbage for emoji,
      while a proper one round-trips them.
    </p>

    <h2>Put it into practice</h2>
    <p>
      The{' '}
      <a href="/tools/base64">Base64 Encode / Decode</a> tool encodes text or a whole file, supports the
      URL-safe alphabet and 76-character MIME wrapping, and round-trips UTF-8 correctly. For the related
      encodings, try <a href="/tools/base32">Base32</a>,{' '}
      <a href="/tools/hex">Hex</a> or the <a href="/tools/url-encode">URL encoder</a>. And if you
      are hashing rather than encoding, the{' '}
      <a href="/guides/which-hash">Which hash should I use?</a> guide covers the difference.
    </p>

    <h2>Rules of thumb</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use it for text-only channels, not as a general container for binary data.</li>
      <li>
        Never treat it as privacy — an encoded value is one paste into a decoder away from readable.
      </li>
      <li>Pick the URL-safe alphabet whenever the value might land in a URL or a filename.</li>
      <li>
        Prefer hex for anything a human will read or diff, such as digests and checksums: it is
        case-insensitive and contains no punctuation.
      </li>
      <li>
        When decoding a pasted value, tolerate stray whitespace and missing padding on input, but be
        strict when turning a file back into bytes.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>More guides</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/which-hash">Which hash should I use?</a> — digests, password storage and HMAC.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/cron-cheat-sheet">Cron syntax cheat sheet</a> — scheduling recurring jobs without
        guessing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/color-contrast-wcag">Colour contrast and WCAG</a> — accessible colour choices.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
